WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.688 --> 00:00:14.610
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.510
CHRIS KLEBAN: The Cloud
OnAir, live webinars

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.150
from Google Cloud.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.760
We host these webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.050
And my name is Chris Kleban.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.690
And today, I'll be
talking about cloud GPUs.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.700
You can ask questions
any time on the platform,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.385
and we have Googlers
standing by to answer them.

00:00:35.385 --> 00:00:36.260
So let's get started.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.710
So if you're out
in the audience,

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.305
live or watching
the recording, let

00:00:43.305 --> 00:00:44.680
me just spend a
couple of minutes

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.950
talking about what a GPU
is, in case you don't know.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.550
So as this picture shows,
GPUs started with gaming.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.380
My mom would play
Frogger back when

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.860
I was a little baby, trying to
get that frog across the road

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:58.420
or over the river.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.180
I played Nintendo,
Super Mario Bros.,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.140
going down the pipes
and the secret warps.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.930
And my son now plays Minecraft
on his Xbox with his friends.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.640
And over the time
of these generations

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.890
of gaming in my
family, the graphics

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.487
have gotten better and better.

00:01:13.487 --> 00:01:15.070
And those graphics
have gotten better,

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.069
thanks to a little hardware
chip called the GPU.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.180
These GPUs are everywhere.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.130
They're in your cell
phones, and your laptops,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.990
and your game consoles, and
your PCs under your desk.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.330
And for data centers
and Cloud, what we do

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.810
is we take that small GPU
and make a really big one.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.700
And that big GPU--

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.400
you'll see a picture of
three of them stacked one

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.690
on top of the other--

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.580
we put them in servers.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.120
And like GPUs do for gaming,
GPUs in the data center

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.250
do for computing.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:50.430
Gaming needs lots
of things drawn

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.190
on the screen in real-time,
scientific research needs

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:59.190
lots of math done as fast
and as quickly as possible.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.030
So how does GPU
acceleration compute work?

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.220
Well, you take some
application code--

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.950
it can be written in
Python, C++, TensorFlow,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.270
anything you want--

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.780
and all that code
mostly runs on the CPU.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.100
The CPU does things
sequentially,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.760
one after the time.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.989
There's an if-then logic.

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:18.530
But what if your
application code has

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.570
a bunch of math it wants to do?

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:21.960
Instead of doing
it one at a time

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.660
and taking a long
time, what if you do

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.000
that math all at the same time?

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.110
So the code in the
green, the math part,

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:32.040
let's ship that to the
GPU that could do it fast.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.850
Let's multiply 10 numbers
together, 100, a million,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:38.060
a billion, get it done
quickly, put the results back

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.290
to the CPU.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.270
And in essence, that's
GPU accelerated compute.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.140
Together, it's speeding
up your applications.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.630
So what kind of workloads
are we talking about here?

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.750
The first one's machine
learning, right?

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.810
GPUs are all about machine
learning and AI these days.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.940
And there's two types
of workloads-- training

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.050
and inference.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.130
Training is about building the
artificial intelligence logic,

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.050
birthing it, if you will, right?

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.930
It's not a person saying,
well, if the car sees a human,

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.180
you better hit the brakes,
you better turn left,

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.560
you better turn right.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.130
It's not about that at all.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:15.260
It's about putting a bunch
of data, synthetic or actual,

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.160
through some logic, having
the computer system learn what

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.930
the right behaviors are or learn
how to find a cat in a photo

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.162
and build that
AI's intelligence.

00:03:26.162 --> 00:03:27.620
And then machine
learning inference

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.120
is, once you have
that intelligence,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.020
it's doing something with it.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.590
Google Photos is this
great app, for example.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.150
And it does search, right?

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.920
I have 100,000
pictures of my kids,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.690
but it's really hard to find the
picture that I'm looking for.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.730
Well, on Google
Photos, I can just

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.060
click the picture of my
son, Aiden, type in the word

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:52.490
"beach", and it predicts
and finds really quickly,

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:56.160
with accelerated hardware, all
the pictures I'm looking for.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.790
And a user doesn't want to
wait five minutes, 10 minutes

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.020
to find that, they
want it automatically.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.610
And that's what inference is--
using the AI to do something.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.540
GPU acceleration is also great
for HPC, or high performance

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.890
computing workloads.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.020
Here, we're talking about
weather simulations,

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.380
engineering, seismic analysis,
a bunch of things that

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.800
typically run on grid systems.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.200
And then there's the
classic visualization case.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.870
It could be gaming, or it could
be a working professional that

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.660
might have a workstation
under their desk

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.450
with expensive GPU hardware.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.000
And maybe the cloud GPU is
a better approach there.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.840
So those are a few
workloads done by GPUs.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.450
So let's talk about one
of the first 99 problems,

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.400
but a cloud GPU ain't one.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.990
The first problem is about
access to compute power.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:48.229
So we asked a bunch of AI
researchers and experts,

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:50.270
what are the things that
contribute to all the AI

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:51.740
advances?

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.440
80% of them said, one
of the main reasons

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.760
was just access to raw,
high power compute.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.094
That's amazing, right?

00:05:00.094 --> 00:05:02.510
It's not necessarily that we're
learning to do new things,

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.310
it's just access to
more power that's

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:06.590
really driving things forward.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:09.140
On the other hand,
we asked people

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:12.740
coming to TensorFlow
organization, which

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.340
is an open source machine
learning algorithm creation

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.580
framework, we asked them,
what are the problems you

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.154
have with building AI?

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:22.820
And a large majority
of them said, well,

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:25.400
we just don't have access
to the compute we need.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.410
So here we have this problem.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.540
Access to compute
drives innovation.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:32.107
People trying to innovate
don't have access to compute.

00:05:32.107 --> 00:05:33.440
So there has to be a better way.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:40.130
So according to an
article on time.com,

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.590
if you wanted to buy
a supercomputer, one

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.930
of the largest
computers in the world,

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:50.540
it might cost you $100
million or $250 million.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.340
That's a lot of money.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.910
On the top 500 list, which
lists the biggest supercomputers

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:59.850
in the world, back in 2008,
the number one supercomputer

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:04.730
had 1,456 teraflops.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.491
That's a lot of power.

00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:08.240
But imagine if you're
a researcher working

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.330
on a really important
business problem, like I want

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.640
to get some recommendations
for online shopping,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.340
or I want to innovate
with self-driving cars.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.540
And let's say you went to your
CTO, your director, and said,

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:24.160
can I please buy a
supercomputer for $100 million?

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.810
You would be laughed at not
because the business problem

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:31.030
isn't real, just that spending
that capital upfront is crazy.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.670
So let's talk about
the alternatives.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:39.520
Let's say we take 1,000
K80 GPUs on Google Cloud

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:41.890
and created a
cluster for an hour.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:45.190
Using regular, on-demand pricing
it would cost you about $500

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.250
for those 1,000 GPUs
to run an experiment,

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.620
to see if what you're
working on will do the thing

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.439
you need for your business.

00:06:52.439 --> 00:06:54.980
And if you use something called
preemptible GPUs, which we'll

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.020
talk about later, you
could even drive down

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.440
that cost even more to
$250 an hour or less.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.990
So now let's say you have
the same conversation

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.649
with your IT director,
who manages the budget,

00:07:05.649 --> 00:07:07.940
and say, I want to solve this
really important business

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.020
problem.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:11.120
I want to do some
quick innovation

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.220
tests to see if this
is going to work.

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:17.420
Do you all mind if I spin up
this cluster, a super compute

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.930
worth of power, spend $250?

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.060
Well, you'll be
laughed at again,

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.010
but not because they're
going to say no.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:29.040
They're going to say, of
course, just go do that.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.400
So that's the power
of Google Cloud--

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.670
bringing you scale of compute.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.690
And let's go ahead
and show you a demo

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:40.230
of what it looks like to spin up
a cluster of compute instances.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.290
So what I'm showing
you now, this

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:44.990
is the Google Cloud console.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:47.340
Google Cloud, if you go
over to the top left,

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.610
you'll see there's a
lot of services here.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.216
There's VM instances,
instance groups.

00:07:52.216 --> 00:07:54.090
And what we are, we're
in the Compute Engine.

00:07:54.090 --> 00:07:56.770
This is all about
infrastructure as a service.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.080
And what I'll show you is
how to do an instance group.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.970
And what an instance group
is, is a logical entity

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:03.210
of infrastructure.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:06.800
It could be one server, it could
be 1,000, it could be 10,000.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.840
And you use those
instance groups

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.570
to run a series of workloads.

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:13.850
You can scale it
up, scale it down.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.870
You could have Google
auto-scale it, based on load.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:20.490
So to create an instance
group, click on a button,

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.270
give it a name.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:26.480
We're going to call
this Cloud OnAir 5.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:32.260
We are going to pick a single
zone, because we only care

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.690
about doing this experiment.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.526
We're going to pick a region.

00:08:37.526 --> 00:08:38.900
Plenty of regions
to choose from.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:44.202
We'll pick Asia East One,
Asia East One A, as a zone.

00:08:44.202 --> 00:08:45.910
And there's two kind
of instance groups--

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:49.311
a managed instance group, and
an unmanaged instance group.

00:08:49.311 --> 00:08:51.560
Managed instance group, we
can do a lot of things for.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:54.370
We could do rolling updates,
roll your code forward,

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:55.060
roll it back.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:57.220
We could do auto-scaling,
load balancing.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.870
Now, for this demo,
we'll pick this.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.980
For instance templates,
this is where

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.120
you go ahead and pick a template
to create a virtual machine.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.380
And here, you're going to pick
the size of the VM, the shape,

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:11.010
and all of that.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.930
So we're going to go ahead and
customize an instance template.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:19.520
We'll pick the number of CPUs
we want, anywhere from 1 to 96.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.570
We'll pick the amount
of memory we want,

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.720
and we'll go look at GPUs.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:28.790
In this case, we
want one GPU per VM.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:33.010
And since this is a demo,
let's just do one core.

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:36.620
And let's do four
gigabytes of RAM.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:39.560
Let's do one GPU.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.290
Let's give it an
operating system.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.450
Here, there's plenty
to choose from--

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:47.300
application images, OS
images, custom images.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.390
In this case, we'll
just pick Ubuntu

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:56.601
with a standard
disc or an SSD disc.

00:09:56.601 --> 00:09:58.850
Maybe this is machine learning,
so we want to go fast.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:02.180
And we'll pick a 10
gigabyte SSD back disc.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:04.880
We'll save this image,
or this template,

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:06.350
called instance template one.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:14.230
So now that we have this
template, all we need to do

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:17.380
is create 1,000 copies of this.

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:18.880
So we'll go down
to Auto-scaling.

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:20.379
We'll turn this
off, because we want

00:10:20.379 --> 00:10:22.740
to create a static cluster.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.330
And for this demo, we'll just
put number of instances as 1

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.400
for now.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:30.900
And we'll hit create.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:35.610
So now Google Cloud's creating
this logical group in Asia

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:37.550
with one VM.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:41.010
It's going to give each of those
VMs an IP address, an SSH key.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:45.040
It's going to secure it in
your own private network.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:48.924
It's going to have NVIDIA
drivers installed with CUDA.

00:10:48.924 --> 00:10:50.714
Depending on your
application, you

00:10:50.714 --> 00:10:52.630
can go ahead and pre-install
your application,

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:55.420
like TensorFlow, or
whatever you need.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.250
If you click on the
logic group, you'll

00:10:57.250 --> 00:11:00.080
see that there's one
instance installed.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:02.680
It has an IP
address, an external.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:05.260
You can go over to the
Details tab and look

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:07.190
at what kind of instance
template was used,

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:09.790
whether or not
auto-scaling was on.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:11.410
You can go over to
Monitoring to see

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.960
things like how
much CPU utilization

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:18.620
your cluster is using, or the
size of the cluster over time,

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.554
and so on and so forth.

00:11:20.554 --> 00:11:21.970
If you go to the
instance, you can

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:26.650
go in and SSH to the instance
that was just created.

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:28.730
And since we want
a supercomputer,

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:31.519
we want 1,000 VMs all at once.

00:11:31.519 --> 00:11:34.060
We want to run that experiment
really quickly-- super compute

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:35.240
power.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:39.740
We don't want one,
we want 10,000.

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:42.890
So I'm going to change the
number of instances to 1,000,

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:44.844
and I'm going to hit Save.

00:11:44.844 --> 00:11:46.510
And now what Google
Cloud's going to do,

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:48.010
it's going to spin
up and it's going

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:51.470
to create 1,000
instances, all of them

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:55.110
with the infrastructure I need,
the applications installed.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:57.470
And I'm going to be able to
use that for my workloads.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:11:59.260
It's going to take
a few minutes.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.370
And for the sake of
time, we'll keep going.

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.660
But just like that,
you get a supercomputer

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:08.925
in a matter of minutes
for an affordable price.

00:12:08.925 --> 00:12:09.800
So let's switch back.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:16.260
So we talked a little
bit about scale.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:18.830
We talked about GPUs,
why they're important.

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:21.050
Now, let's double click
on AI a little bit.

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:23.295
Businesses no longer
have the option just

00:12:23.295 --> 00:12:26.120
to skip artificial intelligence
and machine learning.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.290
It's innovate or die.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:30.870
How can I differentiate
myself from the competitors?

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:32.060
How can I reduce costs?

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:33.590
How can I do something new?

