you're going to have
cores of memory that

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:42.070
goes along with that.

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:43.690
No option, otherwise.

00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:47.020
Or if you want four P100s
with another provider,

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:50.380
you'll have to get the
cores and the memory that

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:52.370
go along with that.

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:56.360
And because of that, your price
per month is going to increase.

00:24:56.360 --> 00:25:01.820
This is showing you
$4,455 per month for this.

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:03.820
So the first way
to save money is

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:06.970
we get something called
sustained usage discount.

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:09.280
So just by using us for
a long period of time

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:11.740
for maybe a workload that
keeps going and going,

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:13.240
not just for one
VM, but think of it

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:15.000
as for the
infrastructure, if you use

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:18.490
a GPU or a VM for a while,
we just automatically

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:20.350
give you discounts--

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:22.740
up to 30% for that month.

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:23.890
It's this amazing saving.

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:27.110
You don't have to do a
contract, just save right away.

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:28.770
So that's one option.

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:33.460
Another way to save money on GPU
compute is the customization.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:36.580
Well, let's say I don't need
62 cores with my four GPUs,

00:25:36.580 --> 00:25:38.050
because maybe my
workload does all

00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:40.700
the pre-processing elsewhere.

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:44.020
So let's just bring back
down the number of cores.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:48.370
And for host memory, well, we
only use the GP memory, right?

00:25:48.370 --> 00:25:48.975
That's faster.

00:25:48.975 --> 00:25:49.570
It's quicker.

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:51.028
We don't need a
lot of host memory,

00:25:51.028 --> 00:25:52.970
so let's bring that down.

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:55.670
So just by sliding this down,
because maybe your application

00:25:55.670 --> 00:25:58.020
doesn't need it, we've
saved even more money.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:02.420
We took it from roughly
$4,300 to $3,100.

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:03.430
How about another way?

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:06.380
Let's give you another option
to save money on Google Cloud.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:09.290
So we have something
called preemptible VMs.

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:14.090
And if I just go down
here, click on Management,

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:17.660
there's an option, do you
want a preemptible or not?

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:24.000
If I say yes, now I'll notice
I just saved another $1,000.

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:26.660
And what a preemptible
VM is, Google's

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:31.370
spare capacity that we might
not be able to sell elsewhere.

00:26:31.370 --> 00:26:34.370
If you're willing to use it
with certain limitations,

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:35.910
you can save more money.

00:26:35.910 --> 00:26:37.970
So there are a wide
range of areas on how

00:26:37.970 --> 00:26:40.800
you can save on GPU instances.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:44.690
So let's switch back and
tell you a little bit more

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:46.845
about cloud GPUs.

00:26:46.845 --> 00:26:49.480
So let's double-click
on Preemptible GPUs.

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:51.600
I kind of hinted
at them just now.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.170
And what they are, it's
a win-win for everybody.

00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:57.680
Google Cloud might have a
little extra GPU capacity.

00:26:57.680 --> 00:26:59.840
And what we do is we
expose them to you

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:02.020
through preemptible GPUs.

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:03.740
Now there's a limitation.

00:27:03.740 --> 00:27:05.810
The limitation is the
VM itself can only

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:08.120
last for up to 24 hours.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:11.240
And we might have to take
that VM away from you

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:12.950
if someone else needs it.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:17.480
But in exchange for that, the
CPU and memory will be 80% off,

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:21.330
and the GPUs will be 50% off.

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:23.230
That's just a clear
way to win-win.

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:25.710
So if you have a batch workload,
like a big training job,

00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:28.440
and you want to save some money,
go ahead and create a managed

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:29.790
instance group.

00:27:29.790 --> 00:27:33.660
Say you want 1,000
VMs with V100 GPUs.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:36.180
And if we have the
capacity, you'll get it.

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:39.060
And write your code,
let's say TensorFlow code,

00:27:39.060 --> 00:27:42.300
to do automatic checkpointing
where, as it's doing the work,

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.360
it saves the work, so that
if the VM comes back online

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.510
once we have capacity, it just
takes over and picks back up.

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:51.070
So if you're willing to
automate some of your work,

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:53.250
you can save some money
through preemptible GPUs.

00:27:58.003 --> 00:28:02.270
So what about it being
hard to use GPUs?

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:05.769
Well, I guess it's
harder than using CPUs.

00:28:05.769 --> 00:28:07.310
It's harder in the
sense where you're

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:10.970
going to write some code, or
you download an application,

00:28:10.970 --> 00:28:12.680
and in the code
sense, maybe you have

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:14.870
to write some new
TensorFlow code.

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:17.400
Just say, don't
use CPU, use GPU,

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:20.200
and TensorFlow takes
care of the rest.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:22.610
Or if you have an
HPC software, just

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:24.605
get it that it's
GPU-enabled, and it

00:28:24.605 --> 00:28:26.584
will work right out of the box.

00:28:26.584 --> 00:28:27.750
But what about Google Cloud?

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:30.050
What do we do to make it easier?

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.330
So the first thing,
we integrate GPUs

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:34.940
across the stack of services.

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:37.100
So far, I've showed
you Compute Engine,

00:28:37.100 --> 00:28:41.090
about how getting VMs with
GPUs is quick and easy.

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:43.370
But it's also integrated
with Container Engine.

00:28:43.370 --> 00:28:45.770
Go ahead and get your
cluster of container

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:48.515
enabled Kubernetes engine
machines with GPUs,

00:28:48.515 --> 00:28:50.810
and we'll run your docker
or container workflows

00:28:50.810 --> 00:28:52.550
through there.

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:55.250
Cloud Machine Learning
Engine, the managed service

00:28:55.250 --> 00:28:58.190
for training and inference
machine learning, that's

00:28:58.190 --> 00:28:59.387
also integrated with GPUs.

00:28:59.387 --> 00:29:01.470
So you don't have to deal
with the infrastructure.

00:29:01.470 --> 00:29:04.940
You don't have to deal with
the complexities of building

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:06.920
from source TensorFlow
and things like that.

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:08.990
You just want to run your
job, so just go ahead

00:29:08.990 --> 00:29:10.760
and work in code.

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:14.690
Or Dataproc, Hadoop and
Spark clusters as a service,

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.977
also give you VMs and
infrastructure with GPUs.

00:29:17.977 --> 00:29:20.060
And you don't have to worry
about the complexities

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:21.650
of managing that.

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:25.030
And then the fifth service
with integrate GPUs is Zync.

00:29:25.030 --> 00:29:27.890
Zync is our cloud video
broadcasting service

00:29:27.890 --> 00:29:29.750
with a wide range
of things, as far

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:32.040
as transcoding or rendering.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:35.100
And it's powered, if
you want, by GPUs.

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:41.840
So let's look at another
problem out there.

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:43.940
Let's say your workload
really demands the highest

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:45.410
performance.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:50.720
And then you say, I could
run CPUs, I could run a K80.

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:52.670
Well, Google has you
covered there too.

00:29:52.670 --> 00:29:56.770
We've just launched
the Tesla V100 GPU.

00:29:56.770 --> 00:29:59.120
This is the latest
GPU from NVIDIA.

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:04.460
It has up to a petaflop of
performance in a single VM.

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:09.890
And that petaflop of performance
in one VM comes from eight GPUs

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.510
and with mixed precision.

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:13.430
And this mixed
precision means when

00:30:13.430 --> 00:30:16.930
you're doing a math that's maybe
not super, super accurate--

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:18.790
and engineering
simulations need that,

00:30:18.790 --> 00:30:21.410
but machine learning
workloads don't.

00:30:21.410 --> 00:30:24.030
So it's great for that.

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:26.780
V100s also come with NV Link.

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:29.450
And what that means is we
have a special hardware board

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:31.420
where our eight GPUs
are connected together.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:34.760
And on that hardware board,
we use NV Link 2.0 technology

00:30:34.760 --> 00:30:38.390
that gives 300 gigabytes
per second of bandwidth

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:40.280
between these GPUs.

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:42.770
So in a way, it kind
of creates a system

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:45.500
where it's such high
performance between these GPUs,

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:49.100
you could think of a
collection of GPUs as one.

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:53.870
You could use more GPU memory
for fast ML and HPC workloads.

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:56.900
You could transfer data between
these GPUs really quickly.

00:30:56.900 --> 00:31:01.520
And you'll notice that, if
you just scale out by saying,

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:04.880
give me 1,000 VMs with one
GPU each, that definitely

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:06.600
gets you done faster.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:08.180
But as you go,
please give me one

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:12.740
VM with NV Link high speed
network with multiple GPUs.

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:15.320
The benefits there
are even better.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:19.370
We've noticed that up to
40% faster performance just

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.760
for your deep learning, machine
learning, or HPC workloads,

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:25.590
just by turning on an
NV Link on the V100.

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:28.760
So it's a super fast,
super quick GPU,

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:30.710
and we just launched it.

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:33.930
Try it out.

00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:36.330
So it's not just about GPUs.

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:38.430
We want you to pick
Google Cloud because we

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:42.120
want you to see us as the
leaders in infrastructure.

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:44.120
We were very early
with Skylake CPUs

00:31:44.120 --> 00:31:46.650
on Google Cloud that
literally worked with almost

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:48.570
any workload imaginable--

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:50.209
web apps, databases.

00:31:50.209 --> 00:31:51.750
They don't do great
on visualization,

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:53.430
but they're not meant to.

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:55.740
So then if you want to get
a little bit more specific,

00:31:55.740 --> 00:31:58.170
but still generic, use GPUs.

00:31:58.170 --> 00:32:01.680
Machine learning, HPC, video
transcoding, movie rendering,

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:05.310
visualization, they speed
up all of those workloads.

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:07.950
And then we also have
the TPU, a special chip

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:10.100
just for machine
learning workloads.

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:12.382
TensorFlow, the library
for machine learning

00:32:12.382 --> 00:32:14.340
that's open sourced,
works across all of those.

00:32:17.494 --> 00:32:19.410
So we talked a lot about
GPUs and the benefits

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:20.617
of Google Cloud.

00:32:20.617 --> 00:32:22.200
Let's just briefly
talk about a couple

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:25.200
of customers that
use Cloud GPUs.

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:27.307
The first one is Schlumberger.

00:32:27.307 --> 00:32:28.890
Schlumberger is one
of the leading oil

00:32:28.890 --> 00:32:30.680
and gas [? services ?] company.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:33.100
And as this pictures show,
one of the services they do

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:36.260
is they have these sensor
units floating in the ocean.

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:39.540
And they send a whole bunch
of signals down to the ground,

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:42.480
and they get a lot of
data-- sonar, radar,

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:43.799
a bunch of things.

00:32:43.799 --> 00:32:45.840
And they just collect that
data, and they ship it

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:47.730
to Google Cloud.

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:51.280
Google Cloud, they have a
cluster of infrastructure

00:32:51.280 --> 00:32:53.230
with NVIDIA GPUs.

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:55.230
And they process that data.

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:57.280
And the end result of
processing that data

00:32:57.280 --> 00:32:59.320
and doing lots and
lots of math is

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:01.000
they're able to create a map.

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:04.240
And that map will show
them and their customers

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:07.580
how deep the ocean is,
where might there be oil,

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:09.910
where might there
be sand or rock

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:11.680
and how would you get to it.

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:15.550
And those HPC clusters with
on-demand provisioning,

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:18.460
versus on-premise high
capital expenditures

00:33:18.460 --> 00:33:21.430
was one of the reasons that
drove them to Google Cloud,

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:24.670
in addition to our
security, our reliability,

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:29.030
and our partnership with
AI services moving forward.

00:33:29.030 --> 00:33:32.040
Let's look at one more, Shazam.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.530
So I'm really, really
bad at music trivia.

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:39.480
When I go camping with my wife
and friends, we'll put the iPod

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:40.310
or iPad up.

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:41.790
We'll shuffle some songs.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:44.610
And my wife will just
dominate me in this game.

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:46.270
So I love Shazam.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:49.204
Shazam's an app where, if
you hear a song on the radio,

00:33:49.204 --> 00:33:51.120
and you don't know what
it is, or at the mall,

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:53.280
you pull the app out,
you hit a button,

00:33:53.280 --> 00:33:56.640
and it listens to the song, and
it tells you very quickly what

00:33:56.640 --> 00:33:58.890
the name and the artist is,
and lets you listen to it,

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:00.810
and things like that.

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:02.780
And what they do
on Google Cloud is

00:34:02.780 --> 00:34:06.300
they do a lot of data
processing with GPUs

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:08.520
and to make those really
quick predictions of,

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:10.760
hey, I heard the song,
it's probably this song,

00:34:10.760 --> 00:34:12.679
or it is the song.

00:34:12.679 --> 00:34:17.460
They load that information
on databases, on GPUs,

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:20.140
to give very quick
inference workload results.

00:34:24.030 --> 00:34:31.280
So in short, 99 problems,
but a cloud GPU ain't one.

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:33.960
I hope you get started
with Cloud GPU soon.

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:35.570
If you're new to
Google Cloud, you

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:38.330
can sign up, get a
free credit, $300,

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:40.790
and sign yourself
up for some GPUs.

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:42.482
There's a couple of links here.

00:34:42.482 --> 00:34:43.940
And the documentation
will show you

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:46.580
how to create a GPU with a VM.

00:34:46.580 --> 00:34:50.510
And then there's also
information about preemptible.

