00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:06.310
Another good question.

00:38:06.310 --> 00:38:10.890
So you can, yes, in both
GCE and Kubernetes engine.

00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:14.190
So on GCE, there's two
types of instance groups--

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:16.630
a managed instance
group, and an unmanaged.

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:18.420
In the managed
instance group, we

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:21.300
do things like auto-scaling
and rolling back code.

00:38:21.300 --> 00:38:23.280
And with a managed
instance group,

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:27.480
we want the same type of VM,
the same shape and so on and so

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:28.330
forth.

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:32.520
So for that, we say it has to
be the same kind of template.

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:34.920
But an unmanaged instance
group, there, you

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:36.869
can put whatever VMs
you want into a group.

00:38:36.869 --> 00:38:37.785
It could be dedicated.

00:38:37.785 --> 00:38:39.990
It could be preemptible.

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:44.187
And it could scale as you need.

00:38:44.187 --> 00:38:46.770
Then the third way is you could
just have two instance groups.

00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:48.436
You could have a
managed instance group,

00:38:48.436 --> 00:38:51.390
both managed instance group,
one dedicated, one preemptible.

00:38:51.390 --> 00:38:53.490
And you could have one
capacity change over time

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:56.070
with availability, and the
other one always there.

00:38:56.070 --> 00:38:58.560
So that's what a lot of
our customers do on GCE.

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:00.900
On Kubernetes engine, you
have something similar.

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:03.560
Kubernetes engine has
the notion of node pools.

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:06.420
And you could have one pool
of nodes be preemptible,

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:08.760
and another node
pool being dedicated.

00:39:08.760 --> 00:39:11.290
And you can scale that
side differently, as well.

00:39:11.290 --> 00:39:14.688
So both of them have an
option to do it, yes.

00:39:14.688 --> 00:39:16.440
Let's look at the next question.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:18.960
What would I need to do
to use preemptible GPUs

00:39:18.960 --> 00:39:21.480
at large scales on GCE?

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:24.020
So with GCE, the demo
I just showed you,

00:39:24.020 --> 00:39:25.500
that's one way--
a managed instance

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:30.750
group, you create a template,
and you scale up the instance

00:39:30.750 --> 00:39:32.027
group to the size you want.

00:39:32.027 --> 00:39:34.110
In order to get that, there
are a couple of things

00:39:34.110 --> 00:39:35.580
you have to do first.

00:39:35.580 --> 00:39:37.770
You have to have an
account, and then you

00:39:37.770 --> 00:39:39.390
need what's called quota.

00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:42.030
Quota is a way to make sure
that the customers that

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:45.540
are using capacity are the ones
that are the right customers

00:39:45.540 --> 00:39:47.950
to use so in some way.

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:50.340
So at first, you'll have
a small amount of quota.

00:39:50.340 --> 00:39:53.420
And if you really want to
run a cluster of 100 GPUs,

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:57.150
or 1,000, or even more, just go
online, go to the quota system

00:39:57.150 --> 00:39:59.460
and say, this is the amount,
the max I want to run.

00:39:59.460 --> 00:40:01.293
Make a submission, and
we'll get back to you

00:40:01.293 --> 00:40:03.390
shortly with what you're
allowed to go up to.

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:06.990
So those two things, managed
instance group and quota,

00:40:06.990 --> 00:40:08.490
will get you that scale.

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:10.450
Or same thing with
Kubernetes, right?

00:40:10.450 --> 00:40:13.040
You get a Kubernetes
cluster or get some quota,

00:40:13.040 --> 00:40:15.790
and then you can
get up and running.

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:18.610
Next question-- when should
I look at TPUs or GPUs

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:20.035
for ML workloads?

00:40:20.035 --> 00:40:22.390
Well, that's another
good question.

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:26.795
I think both TPUs and GPUs are
going to be a great fit for ML.

00:40:26.795 --> 00:40:29.230
GPUs are going to
be a little bit more

00:40:29.230 --> 00:40:33.040
generic, kind of work good for
all workloads, all libraries--

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:38.290
TensorFlow, PyTorch, Skylake,
[? Keris. ?] You name it,

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:39.627
it's going to work.

00:40:39.627 --> 00:40:40.960
And so it'll work for inference.

00:40:40.960 --> 00:40:42.400
It's going to work for training.

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.790
TPUs, they're going to be very
specific to the TensorFlow,

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:47.440
for now, library.

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:50.050
And it's also good for
very specific models.

00:40:50.050 --> 00:40:53.530
And it's going to be really
good from a price performance

00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:55.482
perspective for those
models it works.

00:40:55.482 --> 00:40:57.190
So at Google Cloud,
you could use either.

00:40:57.190 --> 00:40:59.600
We hope you do use
the one you want.

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:01.570
And we'll keep launching
new generations

00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:03.160
and for better
price performance.

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:04.743
We'll keep launching
new features that

00:41:04.743 --> 00:41:08.606
make it even easier to
support ML workloads on GCE.

00:41:08.606 --> 00:41:10.230
And last question--
are there any plans

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:13.660
to offer AMD GPUs
on Google Cloud?

00:41:13.660 --> 00:41:16.014
At this time, we
just do NVIDIA GPUs,

00:41:16.014 --> 00:41:17.680
but we're talking to
some customers that

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:18.910
have expressed interest.

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:22.620
So if you have interest
for AMD, just reach out--

